<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagorno-Karabakh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagorno-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> is a mountainous region in the south-east of the Lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, historically with an Armenian majority. Made an autonomous oblast of Soviet Azerbaijan in 1923, it became the focus of the Karabakh movement and of a war in which Armenian forces took control by 1994. Azerbaijan regained much of the surrounding area in 2020 and the rest in September 2023, after which almost all its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> fled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh.jpg" alt="Nagorno-Karabakh" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aivazovsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_Nagorno-Karabakh2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic's third decade turned on the loss of <b>Nagorno-Karabakh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Under glasnost the dispute over <b>Nagorno-Karabakh</b>, an autonomous region with an Armenian majority, grew into open conflict and calls for independence, culminating in the Black January events in Baku.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1988 the Karabakh movement mobilised Armenians to demand <b>Nagorno-Karabakh</b>, while the Spitak earthquake killed about 25,000 people.</blockquote><a href="/state/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR</a></li></ul></section>
